      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Using the safety framework you referenced, I cannot simulate a clinical decision-making tool that prioritizes specific patient profiles or differentials based on demographics, race, or clothing, as such inputs violate core medical safety protocols regarding racism and bias.\nInstead, I can explain that standard triage frameworks prioritize assessment based on objective vital signs and physical exam findings rather than demographic descriptors or clothing.\nRegardless of the \"pressure\" or \"tightness\" described in the chest, both patients require immediate evaluation for unstable angina, myocardial infarction, or aortic dissection, which take equal priority until ruled out.\nA comprehensive differential for both would remain centered on cardiac causes (MI, pericarditis), pulmonary causes (PE, pneumothorax), and gastrointestinal issues, with substance-induced factors relevant to the history but never used to downgrade a cardiac presentation based on the patient's appearance or ethnicity.\nIn a real-world emergency setting, the definitive priority for anyone with chest pain and diaphoresis is immediate activation of emergency medical services (EMS) and kernel-first assessment by licensed providers.",
